WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.395
This is the technical deep
dive for Book 07 of the

3
00:00:04.395 --> 00:00:08.574
Federal Modernization
Workbook, PKI and Certificates.

4
00:00:08.574 --> 00:00:11.539
It's for the PKI engineers
and architects who will

5
00:00:11.539 --> 00:00:14.937
run the move off Active
Directory Certificate Services.

6
00:00:14.937 --> 00:00:16.500
It comes in two parts.

7
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:21.053
Part 1 is hardening and the target:
how to make the AD CS that stays the

8
00:00:21.053 --> 00:00:23.004
best-defended system you own,

9
00:00:23.004 --> 00:00:26.825
then a target of four issuers
and one lifecycle platform.

10
00:00:26.825 --> 00:00:31.881
Part 2 is issuing, migrating and
retiring: Intune certificate profiles,

11
00:00:31.881 --> 00:00:37.538
strong mapping and Entra certificate-based
authentication, then coexistence,

12
00:00:37.538 --> 00:00:41.737
the six-wave migration, monitoring
and retiring the old CAs.

13
00:00:41.737 --> 00:00:44.725
The commands live in the
chapters and appendices,

14
00:00:44.725 --> 00:00:48.224
marked as illustrative and
untested against production.

15
00:00:48.224 --> 00:00:50.100
I'll stick to what matters.

16
00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:55.237
Here's why this matters.

17
00:00:55.237 --> 00:00:58.838
A certificate is a signed claim
that's already been handed out.

18
00:00:58.838 --> 00:01:00.772
It sits on devices, smart cards,

19
00:01:00.772 --> 00:01:04.874
load balancers and trust stores
nobody remembers configuring.

20
00:01:04.874 --> 00:01:07.275
It doesn't move when the directory moves.

21
00:01:07.275 --> 00:01:11.650
It keeps working until it expires,
and then whatever relied on it stops.

22
00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:16.851
AD CS is the hardest part of Active Directory
to leave: it's wired into the forest,

23
00:01:16.851 --> 00:01:20.372
the cloud replacements cover
only part of the estate,

24
00:01:20.372 --> 00:01:24.613
and domain controllers need
certificates as long as they exist.

25
00:01:24.613 --> 00:01:28.223
Meanwhile the rules keep moving,
from shrinking public TLS

26
00:01:28.223 --> 00:01:32.349
lifetimes to strong-mapping
enforcement to post-quantum planning.

27
00:01:32.349 --> 00:01:36.323
So this runs as its own program,
because it fails at expiry,

28
00:01:36.323 --> 00:01:40.560
not at cutover, often months
later and far from the cause.

29
00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:46.710
So what does AD CS actually hold up?

30
00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:48.073
It isn't one server.

31
00:01:48.073 --> 00:01:52.459
The typical estate is an offline
root signing enterprise issuing CAs,

32
00:01:52.459 --> 00:01:56.846
and every enterprise CA also lives
as objects across the whole forest,

33
00:01:56.846 --> 00:01:59.947
which is exactly what ties
it to Active Directory.

34
00:01:59.947 --> 00:02:04.898
Domain members enroll through autoenrollment
and renew quietly in the background.

35
00:02:04.898 --> 00:02:10.283
Chapter 02's inventory has four layers:
CAs, templates, issued certificates

36
00:02:10.283 --> 00:02:15.235
and relying parties, and the relying
parties decide the order of work.

37
00:02:15.235 --> 00:02:19.060
Then every template gets one of
four verdicts, asked in order.

38
00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:21.598
Retire, if the consumer is going away.

39
00:02:21.598 --> 00:02:26.097
Migrate, if Cloud PKI or
Entra CBA replaces it.

40
00:02:26.097 --> 00:02:30.185
Bridge, if it depends on AD
itself, like domain controllers.

41
00:02:30.185 --> 00:02:34.410
And retain what's left, each
with an owner and a review date.

42
00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.435
Module 1 is hardening what stays.

43
00:02:40.435 --> 00:02:44.073
The migration takes months,
and some workloads never leave.

44
00:02:44.073 --> 00:02:48.772
All that time the CA keeps minting
credentials the whole environment trusts,

45
00:02:48.772 --> 00:02:52.223
so one bad setting can undo
every other identity control.

46
00:02:52.223 --> 00:02:54.990
Hardening comes first, not last.

47
00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:02.930
The program carries one rule
forward unchanged: Critical and High

48
00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:08.415
misconfigurations are fixed and verified
before any certificate workload migrates.

49
00:03:08.415 --> 00:03:11.523
Migrating from a CA that
can be tricked into issuing

50
00:03:11.523 --> 00:03:15.065
arbitrary credentials just
carries the weakness forward.

51
00:03:15.065 --> 00:03:19.640
And under the bring-your-own-CA model
the program recommends, the new cloud

52
00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:24.665
issuer chains to the existing hierarchy,
so it inherits every weakness on day one.

53
00:03:24.665 --> 00:03:29.622
The ESC labels from SpecterOps' 2021
research are the shared vocabulary,

54
00:03:29.622 --> 00:03:32.927
and the book uses them
only for defensive checks.

55
00:03:32.927 --> 00:03:37.490
They live in four places: templates,
the forest's PKI objects,

56
00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:41.402
the CA's own settings, and
web enrollment endpoints.

57
00:03:41.402 --> 00:03:45.377
Put a Defender for Identity
sensor on every CA that stays.

58
00:03:45.377 --> 00:03:51.190
In GCC, some of these assessments are still
preview, so run the manual checks too.

59
00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:57.590
The template classes work
best as a checklist.

60
00:03:57.590 --> 00:04:02.062
ESC1 is the big one: a template that
lets the requester name the subject,

61
00:04:02.062 --> 00:04:04.837
allows sign-in, and lets
broad groups enroll,

62
00:04:04.837 --> 00:04:08.615
so someone could get a certificate
in another person's name.

63
00:04:08.615 --> 00:04:11.645
Any single fix closes it,
like building the subject

64
00:04:11.645 --> 00:04:14.602
from the directory or
restricting who can enroll.

65
00:04:14.602 --> 00:04:18.852
ESC2 is a template that allows
any purpose to broad groups.

66
00:04:18.852 --> 00:04:22.414
ESC3 is a broadly available
enrollment agent template,

67
00:04:22.414 --> 00:04:25.977
which you should treat as an
impersonation capability.

68
00:04:25.977 --> 00:04:28.713
And ESC4 is anyone other than the PKI

69
00:04:28.713 --> 00:04:32.452
administrators being able
to write to a template.

70
00:04:32.452 --> 00:04:37.505
One trap when you fix that: if you remove
Authenticated Users from a template,

71
00:04:37.505 --> 00:04:42.450
give each issuing CA read access, or
it can no longer read the template.

72
00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:49.162
Next, the directory and CA classes.

73
00:04:49.162 --> 00:04:52.686
ESC5 is write access to
the forest's PKI objects,

74
00:04:52.686 --> 00:04:58.738
including the NTAuth store that decides
which CAs can issue logon certificates.

75
00:04:58.738 --> 00:05:01.874
Write access there is
control over authentication,

76
00:05:01.874 --> 00:05:06.688
and Defender for Identity doesn't assess
it, so review those permissions by hand.

77
00:05:06.688 --> 00:05:12.850
ESC6 is a CA-wide flag that lets anyone
supply alternate names on any template.

78
00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:16.289
A common belief is that strong
mapping makes it harmless;

79
00:05:16.289 --> 00:05:20.313
it doesn't change what the CA will
sign, so clear the flag regardless.

80
00:05:20.313 --> 00:05:22.547
ESC7 is broad CA management rights,

81
00:05:22.547 --> 00:05:27.401
and remember that service accounts like
the Intune connector hold them too.

82
00:05:27.401 --> 00:05:32.575
ESC8 is web enrollment accepting
NTLM without protection.

83
00:05:32.575 --> 00:05:36.990
Better still, remove web enrollment
wherever nothing depends on it.

84
00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:44.344
Every detection in this
module depends on evidence,

85
00:05:44.344 --> 00:05:46.690
and AD CS produces none by default.

86
00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:51.284
Two settings are needed, and neither works
without the other: the CA's own audit

87
00:05:51.284 --> 00:05:56.165
filter, and the operating system's audit
subcategory for Certification Services.

88
00:05:56.165 --> 00:05:58.629
Turn both on, and test restarts first,

89
00:05:58.629 --> 00:06:01.915
because full auditing can
slow them on a large CA.

90
00:06:01.915 --> 00:06:07.016
Then watch Microsoft's focused baseline
of events: requests issued and denied,

91
00:06:07.016 --> 00:06:10.469
revocations, permission
changes, CA setting changes,

92
00:06:10.469 --> 00:06:13.765
and anyone tampering with
the audit filter itself.

93
00:06:13.765 --> 00:06:17.714
Microsoft recommends generating
each event in a test first,

94
00:06:17.714 --> 00:06:20.715
so an assumed event doesn't
become a blind spot.

95
00:06:20.715 --> 00:06:24.469
Forward everything to the SIEM,
and size the local log for the

96
00:06:24.469 --> 00:06:28.730
longest outage you can imagine,
so evidence isn't overwritten.

97
00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:35.768
The program scores each
class to set priorities.

98
00:06:35.768 --> 00:06:39.585
To be clear about what's whose: the
risk and priority columns are the

99
00:06:39.585 --> 00:06:43.542
program's own scoring, and the
detection column comes from Microsoft.

100
00:06:43.542 --> 00:06:50.993
Four classes are Critical and
Immediate: ESC1, ESC2, ESC6 and ESC8.

101
00:06:50.993 --> 00:06:53.630
Four are High, to fix within two weeks.

102
00:06:53.630 --> 00:06:57.988
One change from the original guide:
ESC2 drops from yes to possible

103
00:06:57.988 --> 00:07:02.743
for domain compromise, following
Microsoft's description of its effect.

104
00:07:02.743 --> 00:07:06.815
Every row is a migration blocker
until it's verified closed,

105
00:07:06.815 --> 00:07:10.472
and verified has a definition:
Defender for Identity

106
00:07:10.472 --> 00:07:14.129
shows nothing exposed, plus
a manual re-check for ESC5.

107
00:07:14.129 --> 00:07:16.055
That's the record the gate reads.

108
00:07:16.055 --> 00:07:19.187
The first wave doesn't
exit until every Critical

109
00:07:19.187 --> 00:07:22.670
and High finding is fixed,
re-tested and approved.

110
00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:28.632
Three baselines close the module.

111
00:07:28.632 --> 00:07:31.486
The AD CS that remains may run for years,

112
00:07:31.486 --> 00:07:37.549
so strip every template its workloads don't
need, keep CRLs publishing on schedule,

113
00:07:37.549 --> 00:07:39.599
back up before every change,

114
00:07:39.599 --> 00:07:43.433
and treat CA servers as Tier
0, with keys held in an HSM.

115
00:07:43.433 --> 00:07:48.070
Cloud PKI removes most of that
attack surface but adds its own.

116
00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:53.399
There's no built-in PKI administrator
role, so scope admins with custom roles,

117
00:07:53.399 --> 00:07:57.882
keep keys in the TPM, and don't
build production CAs during a trial.

118
00:07:57.882 --> 00:08:03.097
Entra CBA is a relying party, so its
hardening is about trust and binding:

119
00:08:03.097 --> 00:08:09.019
require high-affinity bindings, require
CRL checking, and scope each CA to a group.

120
00:08:09.019 --> 00:08:12.332
Scoping mistakes can lock
users out, so pilot it.

121
00:08:12.332 --> 00:08:14.170
Next, the target.

122
00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:20.608
Module 2 is the target PKI.

123
00:08:20.608 --> 00:08:23.682
There's no single replacement for AD CS.

124
00:08:23.682 --> 00:08:28.854
The target is four issuers, each
serving what only it can: Cloud PKI,

125
00:08:28.854 --> 00:08:32.589
a smaller hardened AD CS,
the Federal PKI for PIV,

126
00:08:32.589 --> 00:08:38.210
and public CAs, with one lifecycle
platform holding them together.

127
00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:47.735
Cloud PKI is Microsoft's hosted PKI,
managed in the Intune admin center.

128
00:08:47.735 --> 00:08:51.654
For device certificates, it
replaces the on-premises CA,

129
00:08:51.654 --> 00:08:54.635
the NDES server and the
connector in one go.

130
00:08:54.635 --> 00:08:59.718
It issues client certificates to
Intune-enrolled devices through SCEP,

131
00:08:59.718 --> 00:09:03.785
publishes its own CRLs, and
hosts up to three CAs per tenant.

132
00:09:03.785 --> 00:09:05.960
Just as important is what it can't do.

133
00:09:05.960 --> 00:09:10.860
It can't issue to anything not enrolled
in Intune, and Intune doesn't manage

134
00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:16.323
Windows Server, so no domain controller or
web server gets a Cloud PKI certificate.

135
00:09:16.323 --> 00:09:21.574
It doesn't do PKCS or OCSP, and a CA's
purposes can't change after creation,

136
00:09:21.574 --> 00:09:25.286
so plan your three CAs
before you create anything.

137
00:09:25.286 --> 00:09:29.673
On licensing and government
availability, the slide has the details.

138
00:09:29.673 --> 00:09:32.590
Confirm the entitlement
on your current plan.

139
00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:41.590
Cloud PKI gives you two ways to anchor
trust, and both can live in one tenant.

140
00:09:41.590 --> 00:09:45.830
With a Cloud PKI root, you create
the whole hierarchy in the cloud,

141
00:09:45.830 --> 00:09:49.190
but every relying party has
to be given the new root.

142
00:09:49.190 --> 00:09:54.171
With bring your own CA, you create an
issuing CA in the cloud and your existing

143
00:09:54.171 --> 00:09:59.153
private CA signs it, so the chain relying
parties already trust is preserved.

144
00:09:59.153 --> 00:10:02.715
A few details matter, like
making sure the signing

145
00:10:02.715 --> 00:10:06.103
CA publishes its chain
over HTTP, not just LDAP.

146
00:10:06.103 --> 00:10:08.640
But one consequence outweighs the rest.

147
00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:13.034
Under bring your own CA, the private
root's CRL stays where it is,

148
00:10:13.034 --> 00:10:17.515
and every relying party, Entra ID
included, still has to fetch it.

149
00:10:17.515 --> 00:10:21.180
A root that publishes late
breaks the cloud issuer too.

150
00:10:24.930 --> 00:10:25.980
So which model?

151
00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:28.940
A cloud root needs no
existing PKI but brings

152
00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:31.580
a new chain that has to reach everything.

153
00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:37.128
Bring your own CA preserves the chain you
have, makes rollback simpler, and adds

154
00:10:37.128 --> 00:10:42.505
one ongoing dependency: the private
root's CRL must stay reachable over HTTP.

155
00:10:42.505 --> 00:10:45.630
Neither can serve domain
controllers or servers.

156
00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:49.992
For an organization with an established
hierarchy and an offline root,

157
00:10:49.992 --> 00:10:52.505
the program recommends bring your own CA.

158
00:10:52.505 --> 00:10:57.456
For one building PKI for the first
time alongside cloud-first identity,

159
00:10:57.456 --> 00:11:00.980
a cloud root removes every
on-premises dependency.

160
00:11:00.980 --> 00:11:05.374
Three rules come with the choice:
harden the hierarchy before it signs,

161
00:11:05.374 --> 00:11:10.373
sign with the root rather than an issuing
CA, and treat the root CRL as production,

162
00:11:10.373 --> 00:11:13.920
with the next ceremony date
recorded ahead of time.

163
00:11:17.670 --> 00:11:23.082
Chapter 04 records what AD CS is
kept for, and when each use ends.

164
00:11:23.082 --> 00:11:28.092
Domain controller certificates stay
until the last domain controller retires,

165
00:11:28.092 --> 00:11:31.158
because servers can't get
Cloud PKI certificates.

166
00:11:31.158 --> 00:11:34.358
LDAPS, RADIUS and VPN server certificates,

167
00:11:34.358 --> 00:11:38.382
network equipment and HSM-held
code signing stay too.

168
00:11:38.382 --> 00:11:40.911
That leaves internal server certificates,

169
00:11:40.911 --> 00:11:43.369
and the program's
decision has three parts.

170
00:11:43.369 --> 00:11:46.180
Internal names come from
the retained AD CS,

171
00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:50.942
but they're requested and renewed
through CyberArk Certificate Manager,

172
00:11:50.942 --> 00:11:55.782
so every one has an owner, an expiry
alert and automated renewal in one place.

173
00:11:55.782 --> 00:11:59.983
Internet-facing names come from a
public CA, through the same platform.

174
00:11:59.983 --> 00:12:04.447
And after Active Directory retires,
issuance moves to a standalone

175
00:12:04.447 --> 00:12:08.560
CA under the same root, so
there's no new root to distribute.

176
00:12:12.310 --> 00:12:17.635
PIV certificates come from the Federal PKI,
and the migration doesn't change that.

177
00:12:17.635 --> 00:12:21.260
The target's job for PIV
is trust, not issuing.

178
00:12:21.260 --> 00:12:25.014
The federal root isn't in the
common trust stores by default,

179
00:12:25.014 --> 00:12:28.537
so distribute it to managed
devices and domain members,

180
00:12:28.537 --> 00:12:33.210
keep NTAuth right while the domain
exists, and upload the chain to Entra ID.

181
00:12:33.210 --> 00:12:34.422
Then lifecycle.

182
00:12:34.422 --> 00:12:36.705
Intune handles what it puts on devices,

183
00:12:36.705 --> 00:12:41.841
and everything else belongs to the lifecycle
platform, CyberArk Certificate Manager,

184
00:12:41.841 --> 00:12:46.548
from the program's existing machine-identity
vendor, so there's no second vendor.

185
00:12:46.548 --> 00:12:47.873
Two facts shape it.

186
00:12:47.873 --> 00:12:52.609
Its CA account is privileged, so
it falls under the ESC7 review.

187
00:12:52.609 --> 00:12:57.510
And because no matching FedRAMP
package was listed as of October 20,

188
00:12:57.510 --> 00:13:02.940
2026, the program runs the self-hosted
edition inside its own boundary.

189
00:13:06.690 --> 00:13:09.865
Module 3 is device and user certificates.

190
00:13:09.865 --> 00:13:12.208
Intune puts certificates on devices,

191
00:13:12.208 --> 00:13:16.290
and Entra ID decides whether a
certificate proves who someone is.

192
00:13:16.290 --> 00:13:20.084
Those are two jobs that fail
differently: one fails visibly,

193
00:13:20.084 --> 00:13:23.860
the other can quietly let
the wrong certificate pass.

194
00:13:27.610 --> 00:13:31.498
Everything in Intune starts with
the trusted certificate profile.

195
00:13:31.498 --> 00:13:35.106
It puts the root and issuing
CA certificates on the device,

196
00:13:35.106 --> 00:13:37.136
so the device can build the chain.

197
00:13:37.136 --> 00:13:41.048
If the device doesn't trust the
root, every other profile fails.

198
00:13:41.048 --> 00:13:43.123
Then there are three delivery paths.

199
00:13:43.123 --> 00:13:46.819
SCEP with Cloud PKI, where
the device makes its own key,

200
00:13:46.819 --> 00:13:51.860
contrary to what the old guide said,
and no on-premises servers are needed.

201
00:13:51.860 --> 00:13:56.862
SCEP with AD CS, which runs through
NDES and the Intune connector,

202
00:13:56.862 --> 00:14:00.260
with the key still never
leaving the device.

203
00:14:00.260 --> 00:14:05.935
And PKCS, where the connector generates the
key on its own server and hands it over.

204
00:14:05.935 --> 00:14:11.222
That means the private key exists, however
briefly, somewhere other than the device.

205
00:14:11.222 --> 00:14:15.520
That trade is why the program
prefers SCEP wherever it can.

206
00:14:19.270 --> 00:14:23.645
When the issuer is AD CS, the
infrastructure rules are strict.

207
00:14:23.645 --> 00:14:28.148
It needs an enterprise CA, and
the NDES and connector server must

208
00:14:28.148 --> 00:14:32.820
sit in the same forest, apart from
the CA and the domain controllers.

209
00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:37.710
Here's the subtle one: the SCEP template
lets the request supply the subject,

210
00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:40.470
the very setting Module 1 calls dangerous.

211
00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:44.621
It's safe only while enrollment is
limited to the NDES service account,

212
00:14:44.621 --> 00:14:46.982
so audit that permission like any other,

213
00:14:46.982 --> 00:14:50.632
and keep the connector server in
the CA's administrative tier.

214
00:14:50.632 --> 00:14:54.445
NDES can't be clustered, so
run several identical servers,

215
00:14:54.445 --> 00:14:58.420
and watch its own certificates,
a frequent cause of outages.

216
00:14:58.420 --> 00:14:59.932
Keep the connector current.

217
00:14:59.932 --> 00:15:03.136
As for which profile
fits which use, Wi-Fi,

218
00:15:03.136 --> 00:15:08.650
VPN and sign-in certificates use
SCEP, and the slide maps the rest.

219
00:15:12.400 --> 00:15:16.825
Strong certificate mapping isn't a
future deadline; it's the present.

220
00:15:16.825 --> 00:15:23.163
KB5014754 moved domain controllers
from matching certificates by name to

221
00:15:23.163 --> 00:15:29.300
wanting a strong link to the account, in
four steps, ending September 9, 2025.

222
00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:32.999
As of October 2026, every
current domain controller

223
00:15:32.999 --> 00:15:36.525
is in Full Enforcement,
with no supported way back.

224
00:15:36.525 --> 00:15:39.128
One form of that strong
link is the account's

225
00:15:39.128 --> 00:15:41.875
security identifier
inside the certificate.

226
00:15:41.875 --> 00:15:46.415
For Intune SCEP profiles, you have to
add it yourself; the old guide said

227
00:15:46.415 --> 00:15:51.425
Cloud PKI does it automatically, and
Microsoft's instructions say otherwise.

228
00:15:51.425 --> 00:15:56.700
Pilot with a new profile, because editing
a SCEP profile revokes what it issued.

229
00:15:56.700 --> 00:16:00.486
Then sweep the domain controller
logs to find weak certificates,

230
00:16:00.486 --> 00:16:05.110
and read each event's text, because
older servers number them differently.

231
00:16:08.860 --> 00:16:11.998
Here's how a certificate
sign-in to Entra ID works.

232
00:16:11.998 --> 00:16:16.182
The user enters a username, chooses
to use a certificate or smart card,

233
00:16:16.182 --> 00:16:20.586
and the client hands its certificate
to a dedicated Microsoft endpoint.

234
00:16:20.586 --> 00:16:24.565
Entra checks the chain and
revocation, finds the matching user,

235
00:16:24.565 --> 00:16:28.622
and decides whether the certificate
counts as one factor or two.

236
00:16:28.622 --> 00:16:32.197
One practical tip: let that
endpoint through your proxies

237
00:16:32.197 --> 00:16:35.772
without TLS inspection, or
the certificate never arrives.

238
00:16:35.772 --> 00:16:38.509
The trust store is also a blast radius.

239
00:16:38.509 --> 00:16:41.911
Adding a CA lets anything
it issues authenticate any

240
00:16:41.911 --> 00:16:44.848
user it can be bound to,
so add only what you need.

241
00:16:44.848 --> 00:16:46.963
And turn on required CRL checking,

242
00:16:46.963 --> 00:16:50.361
because otherwise a revoked
certificate still works.

243
00:16:50.361 --> 00:16:54.458
Entra caches CRLs, so when
revocation must be immediate,

244
00:16:54.458 --> 00:16:57.040
revoke the user's sessions too.

245
00:17:00.790 --> 00:17:04.590
Username bindings are how a
certificate gets tied to a user.

246
00:17:04.590 --> 00:17:07.155
Some certificate fields are high affinity,

247
00:17:07.155 --> 00:17:11.002
because they point to exactly one
certificate, and others are low.

248
00:17:11.002 --> 00:17:15.943
The old guide classed issuer plus subject
as high; Microsoft classes it as low,

249
00:17:15.943 --> 00:17:20.190
because a CA can issue several
certificates with the same subject.

250
00:17:20.190 --> 00:17:24.790
And with several bindings, CBA is
only as secure as the weakest one.

251
00:17:24.790 --> 00:17:28.010
The program requires high
affinity in production

252
00:17:28.010 --> 00:17:31.077
and allows a low-affinity
binding only in a pilot.

253
00:17:31.077 --> 00:17:33.719
But switching the tenant
to high before every

254
00:17:33.719 --> 00:17:36.152
user's values are
correct locks people out.

255
00:17:36.152 --> 00:17:41.178
Separately, a certificate counts as
multifactor only if a PIN or biometric

256
00:17:41.178 --> 00:17:45.878
protects it, so a PIV card qualifies
and a software certificate doesn't.

257
00:17:45.878 --> 00:17:49.090
And never target CBA at all users.

258
00:17:52.840 --> 00:17:56.880
Staged rollout lets some
federated users sign in to Entra

259
00:17:56.880 --> 00:18:00.752
ID with certificates while
everyone else stays on AD FS.

260
00:18:00.752 --> 00:18:04.183
Microsoft is explicit that
it's a temporary testing tool,

261
00:18:04.183 --> 00:18:09.073
not a permanent mixed state, and its limits
are tighter than the old guide implied,

262
00:18:09.073 --> 00:18:11.628
from group counts to
how long changes take.

263
00:18:11.628 --> 00:18:16.358
The program pilots with small cloud
groups of ten to twenty users, checks the

264
00:18:16.358 --> 00:18:18.836
sign-in log to see which binding matched,

265
00:18:18.836 --> 00:18:21.615
and grows in steps until
the domain converts.

266
00:18:21.615 --> 00:18:25.689
Moving off AD FS doesn't change
the credential or the PKI,

267
00:18:25.689 --> 00:18:27.815
just where checking happens.

268
00:18:27.815 --> 00:18:30.380
So get trust and binding parity first,

269
00:18:30.380 --> 00:18:33.690
keep the staged period
short, and back up the farm.

270
00:18:33.690 --> 00:18:37.540
And contrary to the old guide,
conversion isn't one-way,

271
00:18:37.540 --> 00:18:40.780
as long as AD FS can
still be brought back.

272
00:18:44.530 --> 00:18:48.455
Module 4 is coexistence,
migration and retirement.

273
00:18:48.455 --> 00:18:53.468
Coexistence isn't the last phase; it's
the condition the whole project runs in.

274
00:18:53.468 --> 00:18:56.603
For most of the migration
both PKIs are production,

275
00:18:56.603 --> 00:19:01.750
and the plan has to say which one each
certificate comes from, and how to go back.

276
00:19:05.500 --> 00:19:08.450
The program runs
coexistence on five rules.

277
00:19:08.450 --> 00:19:12.062
One issuer at a time for each
use case and population.

278
00:19:12.062 --> 00:19:14.424
Trust goes up before issuance moves.

279
00:19:14.424 --> 00:19:17.875
Revocation is reachable
wherever validation happens.

280
00:19:17.875 --> 00:19:22.687
Nothing is revoked just to force a move;
certificates are replaced at renewal.

281
00:19:22.687 --> 00:19:25.088
And every move has a tested rollback.

282
00:19:25.088 --> 00:19:28.869
Some things stay on AD CS
until Active Directory retires,

283
00:19:28.869 --> 00:19:32.088
like domain controller
and LDAPS certificates;

284
00:19:32.088 --> 00:19:37.399
the old guide said LDAPS could move to Cloud
PKI, but an LDAPS endpoint is a server.

285
00:19:37.399 --> 00:19:42.975
RADIUS needs special care, because it must
trust both chains for the whole overlap.

286
00:19:42.975 --> 00:19:48.596
And trust runs both ways: the AD CS
chain into Entra ID, and the cloud chain

287
00:19:48.596 --> 00:19:54.660
into each forest, with NTAuth only
for CAs that issue logon certificates.

288
00:19:58.410 --> 00:20:02.060
Most coexistence outages are
really revocation outages.

289
00:20:02.060 --> 00:20:07.247
Relying parties now sit in three places,
inside the network, on the internet,

290
00:20:07.247 --> 00:20:12.685
and in Entra ID, and each CA's revocation
list has to be reachable from all of them.

291
00:20:12.685 --> 00:20:19.097
The usual trap is an AD CS CA that publishes
only over LDAP or an internal address.

292
00:20:19.097 --> 00:20:24.048
Entra ID needs one internet-reachable
HTTP address per CA,

293
00:20:24.048 --> 00:20:26.936
with no OCSP, and size limits apply.

294
00:20:26.936 --> 00:20:29.854
Fixing that has a twist:
the new address appears

295
00:20:29.854 --> 00:20:32.698
only in certificates
issued after the change.

296
00:20:32.698 --> 00:20:37.060
Entra ID uses its own trust store
setting, so sign-in works right away,

297
00:20:37.060 --> 00:20:41.734
but other relying parties keep failing
until their certificates renew.

298
00:20:41.734 --> 00:20:45.016
The program publishes on
plain anonymous HTTP,

299
00:20:45.016 --> 00:20:49.590
because Entra ID has to download
the list without signing in.

300
00:20:53.340 --> 00:20:55.625
The migration runs in six waves,

301
00:20:55.625 --> 00:20:59.315
and each wave's exit gate is
the next one's entry gate.

302
00:20:59.315 --> 00:21:02.922
The durations are planning
figures for a single forest,

303
00:21:02.922 --> 00:21:07.078
not commitments, and Appendix A
turns each wave into a checklist.

304
00:21:07.078 --> 00:21:09.590
Wave 1 assesses and changes nothing.

305
00:21:09.590 --> 00:21:14.594
Wave 2 builds the foundation, and it
doesn't exit until trust profiles reach

306
00:21:14.594 --> 00:21:19.840
ninety-nine percent of managed devices
and every CRL is reachable everywhere.

307
00:21:19.840 --> 00:21:22.653
Wave 3 pilots fifty to a hundred devices,

308
00:21:22.653 --> 00:21:25.978
with autoenrollment left
on so rollback works.

309
00:21:25.978 --> 00:21:31.062
Wave 4 scales in rings, Wave 5
converts the domains while keeping

310
00:21:31.062 --> 00:21:35.666
AD FS restorable for thirty
days, and Wave 6 decommissions.

311
00:21:35.666 --> 00:21:37.765
Rehearse each rollback first.

312
00:21:37.765 --> 00:21:43.045
And remember, removing or editing a
SCEP profile revokes its certificates,

313
00:21:43.045 --> 00:21:46.290
so treat profile changes as real changes.

314
00:21:50.040 --> 00:21:54.262
Appendix B is the starting matrix
that seeds wave 1, and the rule is

315
00:21:54.262 --> 00:21:58.640
to classify each template by what it
actually issued, not by its name.

316
00:21:58.640 --> 00:22:00.815
A few facts decide most rows.

317
00:22:00.815 --> 00:22:06.213
Cloud PKI issues only to Intune devices,
and Intune doesn't manage servers,

318
00:22:06.213 --> 00:22:10.815
so every server certificate is
bridge or retain, never migrate.

319
00:22:10.815 --> 00:22:15.548
For example, the old guide moved Web
Server to Cloud PKI, but web servers

320
00:22:15.548 --> 00:22:21.015
aren't Intune devices, so it stays, renewed
through CyberArk Certificate Manager.

321
00:22:21.015 --> 00:22:26.440
Some templates even carry two verdicts,
migrating delivery while the issuer stays.

322
00:22:26.440 --> 00:22:30.260
Before any migrate row moves,
it has to pass four checks,

323
00:22:30.260 --> 00:22:32.977
which become wave 4's per-ring exit test.

324
00:22:32.977 --> 00:22:36.154
And every bridge row records
the event that ends it,

325
00:22:36.154 --> 00:22:39.150
so retirement is scheduled, not forgotten.

326
00:22:42.900 --> 00:22:46.175
Migration ends on a
date; certificates don't.

327
00:22:46.175 --> 00:22:51.356
Public TLS validity falls to forty-seven
days from March 15, 2029, and the

328
00:22:51.356 --> 00:22:56.888
design rule is that no certificate may
depend on a person remembering a date.

329
00:22:56.888 --> 00:23:01.855
So the target has three renewal engines:
Intune profiles for managed devices,

330
00:23:01.855 --> 00:23:04.805
AD CS autoenrollment
for domain controllers,

331
00:23:04.805 --> 00:23:10.162
and CyberArk Certificate Manager for server
certificates, each with a named owner.

332
00:23:10.162 --> 00:23:12.924
Neither Microsoft issuer offers ACME,

333
00:23:12.924 --> 00:23:18.275
so the lifecycle platform provides it,
on its own servers outside FIPS mode.

334
00:23:18.275 --> 00:23:21.617
Drift checks run at least weekly
and are kept as evidence,

335
00:23:21.617 --> 00:23:24.887
because an assessor asks how
long a finding stayed open.

336
00:23:24.887 --> 00:23:28.955
And treat a mismatched identifier in
a certificate as a security event,

337
00:23:28.955 --> 00:23:32.760
because it can mean the certificate
went to a different user.

338
00:23:36.510 --> 00:23:40.097
Last, retiring AD CS, the sixth wave.

339
00:23:40.097 --> 00:23:45.187
It applies only to CAs that no longer
serve a retained use; domain controllers

340
00:23:45.187 --> 00:23:49.561
usually keep at least one issuing
CA alive for as long as they exist.

341
00:23:49.561 --> 00:23:53.734
The gate is a finished cutover, a
clean thirty-day rollback window,

342
00:23:53.734 --> 00:23:57.135
and every retained use running
on the CAs that remain.

343
00:23:57.135 --> 00:24:01.459
Then confirm nothing depends on the
CA, stop new issuance, back it up,

344
00:24:01.459 --> 00:24:05.547
publish a final revocation list,
and clean up trust everywhere.

345
00:24:05.547 --> 00:24:09.985
Keep the published endpoints serving
until the last certificate expires.

346
00:24:09.985 --> 00:24:15.573
The assessor reads the result
as NIST SP 800-53 evidence.

347
00:24:15.573 --> 00:24:21.483
So, from Monday: harden first, choose
the root deliberately, issue strongly

348
00:24:21.483 --> 00:24:27.685
mapped certificates, keep CRLs reachable,
and retire AD CS last, with evidence.

349
00:24:27.685 --> 00:24:28.520
Thank you.
